  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1216 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nPersistent upper level troughing continues over much of the\neastern CONUS. The troughing has become a bit less robust in\nanticipation of a developing cut-off low over the southeast\nregion. The low will continue to keep cloud cover our eastern\nzones until it gradually retrogrades northeasterly.\n\nWhile we remain on the back side of the departing cut-off low,\nbenign temperatures will continue through tomorrow. As the low to\nour east becomes displaced, weak southerly flow will emerge on\nWednesday returning temperatures to near normal in the low 90s.\nSoutherly flow will get a bit stronger on Thursday and continue\nto nudge temperatures upwards and transport moisture. Concurrent\nwith an upper level ridge amplifying over the western CONUS, weak\ntroughing will skirt across the Midwest. A few showers may dampen\nour northern zones Thursday, but model soundings continue to show\nlingering dry air aloft which keeps confidence low at this time.\n\nThe weekend into early next week does continue to look hot and\nhumid, but nothing unseasonably warm. Probabilities of heat index\nvalues exceeding 105F (Heat Advisory criteria) remain confined to\nthe MS Delta region and continue to lessen elsewhere\ngeographically. There is uncertainty into how broad or deep the\ntrough will emerge, which will heavily influence temperatures and\nprecipitation this weekend. For now, a 20-40% PoP was carried for\nthe most of the weekend.\n\nDNM",
